---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import EnvGenerator from '../../components/EnvGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title=".env File Generator" description="Turn a simple list of name: value pairs into a properly formatted .env file.">
  <EnvGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why .env formatting still trips people up</h2>
  <p>The .env format looks trivially simple, but subtle mistakes — quoting values inconsistently, stray spaces around the equals sign, or missing newlines — can cause a variable to silently fail to load, which is confusing to debug since the app just behaves as if the variable were unset.</p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your variables as name: value pairs, one per line.</li>
    <li>Copy the generated .env-formatted output.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>Input: <code>DATABASE_URL: postgres://localhost/db</code> becomes <code>DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost/db</code></p>

  <h2>Common .env Formatting Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Adding spaces around the equals sign</strong> (<code>KEY = value</code>) — most .env parsers expect no spaces (<code>KEY=value</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Committing a real .env file to git</strong> — always add it to .gitignore and share a .env.example template instead.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting to quote values containing spaces or special characters</strong>, which some parsers require and others don't — check your specific tooling's expectations.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This .env Generator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Quickly converting a list of config values into .env format</li>
    <li>Generating a starting .env for a new project</li>
    <li>Creating a .env.example template to share with teammates</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>.env File FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Should .env be committed to git?</strong> No — the real .env with actual secrets should always be gitignored; commit only a .env.example with placeholder values.</p>
</ToolLayout>
